\title{Retrocausal Proof Theory:\\Logical Boundaries, Backward Certificates, and Consequence-Guided Search}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{August 1, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study the proposal that a proposition might be established by verifying its logical consequences rather than deriving it directly. For a fixed proposition $P$, the rule asserting that $P$ follows from every verified consequence $Q$ of $P$ is valid if and only if $P$ is already true. An unrestricted rule of this form would prove every proposition. Joint verification guarantees coherence of a finite consequence family, but coherence does not recover the antecedent: the always-true proposition is a verified coherent consequence even of falsehood.

A sound positive theory results from adding a \emph{backward certificate}, an implication from the conjunction of the verified consequences to the candidate proposition. We define a consequence-stable family as one that is both implied by the proposition and jointly sufficient for it, and prove that consequence stability is exactly equivalence between the proposition and the conjunction of the family. For finite candidate spaces, semantic checks produce a survivor set contained in the original space; a failed candidate gives strict cardinality reduction, a passing target is retained, and unique isolation supplies a backward certificate for target equality. Positivity and divisibility by $2$ and $3$ isolate $6$ among the natural numbers below $8$, reducing eight candidates to one. These results distinguish semantic search compression from syntactic proof compression.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Mathematical proof is conventionally presented in the forward direction: axioms and hypotheses are transformed by valid inference rules until the desired conclusion is obtained. Discovery is less linear. A conjecture is often explored through consequences that are easier to compute, compare, or test than the original statement.

This motivates a ``retrocausal'' perspective: infer a theorem from verified consequences that the theorem would entail. The terminology is metaphorical; no temporal reversal is assumed. Deductive validity is stricter than evidential support. From $P\Implies Q$ and $Q$, one cannot generally infer $P$.

We identify an exact boundary. Forward consequence verification alone cannot recover an antecedent. Even joint truth and coherence do not help, because $\True$ passes those requirements for every candidate. Sound recovery requires a backward certificate
\[
(Q_1\land\cdots\land Q_n)\Implies P.
\]
If $P$ also implies each $Q_i$, then $P$ is equivalent to the conjunction. This is consequence stability.

We then separate validity from search. Tests can prune a finite candidate space, and uniqueness can turn the resulting characterization into a certificate. Consequences thereby guide discovery without replacing the implication needed to complete a proof.

\section{Logical framework}

Let $P$ be a candidate proposition and let $\mathcal Q=[Q_1,\ldots,Q_n]$ be a finite list of propositions.

\begin{definition}[Consequence family]
The list $\mathcal Q$ is a family of consequences of $P$ if $P\Implies Q_i$ for every $i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Joint verification]
The family $\mathcal Q$ is jointly verified if every $Q_i$ is true, equivalently if $Q_1\land\cdots\land Q_n$ holds. The empty conjunction is $\True$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Coherence]
The family $\mathcal Q$ is coherent if
\[
\neg\bigl((Q_1\land\cdots\land Q_n)\Implies\False\bigr).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Backward certificate]
A backward certificate for $P$ relative to $\mathcal Q$ is an implication
\[
(Q_1\land\cdots\land Q_n)\Implies P.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Consequence stability]
The family $\mathcal Q$ is consequence-stable for $P$ when $P\Implies Q_i$ for every $i$ and $(Q_1\land\cdots\land Q_n)\Implies P$.
\end{definition}

\section{The boundary of backward confirmation}

\begin{theorem}[Uniform Confirmation Boundary]
For every proposition $P$,
\[
\left[\forall Q,\ ((P\Implies Q)\Implies(Q\Implies P))\right]\Iff P.
\]
Thus confirmation of a fixed $P$ from every verified consequence is available exactly when $P$ already holds.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Assume the uniform rule and choose $Q=\True$. Since $P\Implies\True$ and $\True$ holds, the rule yields $P$. Conversely, if $P$ holds, then the requested conclusion is immediate for every $Q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Universal Collapse]
If
\[
\forall P\,\forall Q,\quad (P\Implies Q)\Implies Q\Implies P,
\]
then every proposition is true.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Fix $P$ and apply the rule with $Q=\True$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No Unrestricted Retrocausal Rule]
No valid universal rule has the form
\[
\forall P\,\forall Q,\quad (P\Implies Q)\Implies Q\Implies P.
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Taking $P=\False$ and $Q=\True$ would derive $\False$ from $\False\Implies\True$ and $\True$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Verification Implies Coherence]
Every jointly verified finite family is coherent.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
If the verified conjunction implied $\False$, applying that implication to the conjunction would produce $\False$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Universal True Control]
For every $P$, the singleton $[\True]$ is a consequence family of $P$, is jointly verified, and is coherent.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Every proposition implies $\True$; $\True$ holds; and $\True\Implies\False$ cannot hold.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Explicit Failure of Recovery]
The proposition $\False$ implies the verified coherent family $[\True]$, but that family has no backward certificate for $\False$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Falsehood implies every proposition. A backward certificate here would be $\True\Implies\False$, which is impossible.
\end{proof}

\section{Backward certificates and stability}

\begin{theorem}[Certified Recovery]
If $\mathcal Q=[Q_1,\ldots,Q_n]$ is jointly verified and $(Q_1\land\cdots\land Q_n)\Implies P$, then $P$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Joint verification supplies the antecedent of the certificate; apply the certificate.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Singleton Two-Way Certification]
If $P\Implies Q$ and $Q\Implies P$, then $P\Iff Q$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The assumptions are exactly the two directions of the biconditional.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[One Complete Consequence]
If $R$ belongs to a jointly verified family and $R\Implies P$, then $P$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Joint verification yields $R$; apply $R\Implies P$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Monotonicity under Verified Extension]
If a base family $\mathcal B$ has a backward certificate for $P$ and every member of $\mathcal B+\mathcal E$ is verified, then $P$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The concatenated verification includes every member of $\mathcal B$, so the base certificate applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Consequence Stability Characterization]
A finite family $[Q_1,\ldots,Q_n]$ is consequence-stable for $P$ if and only if
\[
P\Iff(Q_1\land\cdots\land Q_n).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Stability gives $P\Implies Q_i$ for every $i$, hence the forward implication to the conjunction, and its certificate gives the reverse implication. Conversely, the biconditional gives each pointwise forward implication and the backward certificate.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Conjunctions Form a Stable Class]
The family $[A,B]$ is consequence-stable for $A\land B$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
A conjunction implies each component, and the joint truth of the components is the conjunction.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Verified Stable Families Establish Their Proposition]
If $\mathcal Q$ is consequence-stable for $P$ and jointly verified, then $P$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Apply the backward direction of the stability characterization.
\end{proof}

\section{Finite consequence-guided search}

Let $C$ be a finite subset of a type $X$, and let $\mathcal T=[T_1,\ldots,T_m]$ be Boolean-valued predicates on $X$.

\begin{definition}[Passing and survivors]
A candidate $a$ passes $\mathcal T$ when $T_1(a)\land\cdots\land T_m(a)$ holds. The survivor set is
\[
S(C,\mathcal T)=\{a\in C:T_j(a)\text{ holds for every }j\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Filtering Is Contractive]
For every finite $C$ and every check family $\mathcal T$,
\[
S(C,\mathcal T)\subseteq C,
\qquad
|S(C,\mathcal T)|\le |C|.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Survivor membership includes membership in $C$. Cardinality is monotone under inclusion for finite sets.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict Reduction]
If some $a\in C$ fails at least one check, then
\[
|S(C,\mathcal T)|<|C|.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The failed candidate belongs to $C$ but not to the survivor set, so the inclusion is proper. A proper subset of a finite set has smaller cardinality.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Target Retention]
If $t\in C$ and $t$ passes every check, then $t\in S(C,\mathcal T)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
This is the definition of survivor membership.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unique Survivor Certificate]
If
\[
(T_1(a)\land\cdots\land T_m(a))\Implies a=t,
\]
then the evaluated propositions $T_1(a),\ldots,T_m(a)$ have a backward certificate for $a=t$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The displayed uniqueness implication is exactly that certificate.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Filtering algorithm and information gain}

Scan the candidates. For each candidate, evaluate checks in order and reject immediately after the first failure; retain candidates for which no check fails. For $N=|C|$ and $m$ checks, worst-case time is $O(Nm)$ and output storage is $O(|S|)$. Short-circuiting can lower actual evaluations.

\begin{definition}[Information gain]
When $S(C,\mathcal T)$ is nonempty, define
\[
I(C,\mathcal T)=\log_2\frac{|C|}{|S(C,\mathcal T)|}.
\]
\end{definition}
The contraction theorem gives $I\ge0$. This is semantic compression of a specified candidate universe, not proof length in a calculus.

\section{Arithmetic calibration}

Let
\[
C=\{n\in\mathbb N:n<8\}=\{0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7\}
\]
and define
\[
T_1(n):n>0,\qquad T_2(n):2\mid n,\qquad T_3(n):3\mid n.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Isolation of Six]
The survivor set is
\[
S(C,[T_1,T_2,T_3])=\{6\}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The positive multiples of $2$ below $8$ are $2,4,6$, and only $6$ is divisible by $3$. Conversely, $6$ passes all three checks.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Exact Compression Measure]
The initial cardinality is $8$, the survivor cardinality is $1$, and
\[
\frac{|S|}{|C|}=\frac18,
\qquad I=3\text{ bits}.
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Count the range and singleton and apply the definition of information gain.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Arithmetic Backward Certificate]
For every natural number $n<8$,
\[
(n>0)\land(2\mid n)\land(3\mid n)\Implies n=6.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A passing $n<8$ belongs to the survivor set, which is the singleton $\{6\}$.
\end{proof}

The bound is essential: without it, the checks characterize all positive multiples of $6$ rather than $6$ alone.

\section{Applications and interpretation}

Consequence-guided proof search has a bidirectional architecture. Forward reasoning generates necessary conditions, semantic evaluation prioritizes or discards candidates, and backward certification connects a successful condition set to the original goal. The boundary theorem prevents treating semantic success as deductive completion.

In constraint solving, candidates are assignments and checks are constraints. In program synthesis, examples and partial specifications prune candidate programs, while a final argument against the full specification prevents overfitting. In diagnosis, observations may identify a model only relative to an explicit model class; uniqueness within that class is the backward certificate.

Semantic and syntactic compression must remain separate. A survivor ratio measures contraction of a chosen candidate set. Proof length measures derivation size in a chosen calculus. A large candidate reduction may require a certificate as expensive as a direct proof, while a short structural proof may avoid enumeration entirely. Claims of proof compression must specify the calculus, encoding, inference-node cost, and whether deriving consequences and certificates is counted.

\section{Future work}

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Bounded-arithmetic benchmark.} Fix a sequent calculus for bounds, divisibility, conjunction, and equality. For $N\ge8$, compare a shortest derivation of $n=6$ from $n<N$ with one using $2\mid n$ and $3\mid n$.
\item \textbf{Certificate-cost threshold.} Test whether any universal $c<1$ can bound total certified consequence-guided cost by $c$ times direct proof cost. Counterexamples may force certificates to reproduce direct arguments.
\item \textbf{Information gain and enumeration.} On finite spaces, compare $I=\log_2(|C|/|S|)$ with median candidates inspected. Spaces of size at most $16$ admit exhaustive tests.
\item \textbf{Arithmetic residue certificates.} For distinct primes $p_1,\ldots,p_k$ and $M=\prod_i p_i$, divisibility by every $p_i$ isolates $0$ in $0\le n<M$. Compare balanced certificates with candidate-by-candidate proofs.
\item \textbf{Strict filtering without shortening.} Seek infinite families with unbounded search-space reduction but only additive improvement in shortest proof length.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

Verified consequences cannot by themselves establish the proposition that produced them. Uniform confirmation from arbitrary verified consequences is possible for a fixed proposition precisely when it is already true, and a universal version collapses logic. Coherence does not repair the rule.

A sound method emerges from backward certificates. Consequence stability is equivalence between a target and the conjunction of its listed consequences. In finite search, checks contract candidate sets while retaining passing targets; unique isolation converts this computational reduction into a certificate. The example below $8$ isolates $6$ with three checks and a survivor ratio of $1/8$. Consequences can therefore organize proof search without replacing proof.

